# ==============================================================================
# Arena - Environment Configuration
# ==============================================================================
# Copy this file to '.env' in the same directory and fill in your details:
# cp .env.example .env
#
# The arena reads these on the way up. Anything already exported in the shell
# wins over what is written here, so one `export ARENA_SERVICE_TOKEN=...` can
# still cover the arena, the game and the dugout at once.
#
# HOST and PORT are not here on purpose: they are read by run.sh when it
# launches uvicorn, not by the arena, so they belong in the shell.
# ==============================================================================

# [REQUIRED for the agents] The shared secret a server-side caller carries in
# X-Arena-Service instead of a phone's session cookie. The game's specialist
# agents and the dugout's tools both need the same value, or every tune and
# every shout is refused. Unset authenticates nobody rather than everybody.
ARENA_SERVICE_TOKEN=dev-token

# [SET BEFORE A REAL EVENT] Salts the hash of a player's email, which is how a
# returning player is recognised. It keeps a literal default on purpose: if it
# randomised per start, every hash would change and everyone would lose their
# board history. Changing it later has exactly that effect, so pick one value
# and keep it.
# ARENA_EMAIL_SALT=arena-dev-salt

# [SET BEFORE A REAL EVENT] Signs the session cookies. Unset, the arena mints a
# random one per start, so every phone is logged out by a restart. Never a
# public literal - anything committed here would let anyone forge a session.
# Generate one with: python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"
# ARENA_SECRET=

# The libpq connection string for the Postgres holding rooms, players, seats,
# profiles and event logs. The default reaches a local server over its Unix
# socket: no password, no port, no host. The arena creates the database if the
# server is up but the database is missing, so `brew services start
# postgresql@18` is the whole of the setup. Tests point this at a throwaway of
# their own.
# ARENA_DB=postgresql:///arena

# What the join QR codes encode. A phone on the venue wifi cannot reach the
# laptop's loopback, so a real event sets this to the machine's LAN name or a
# tunnel. The default is right for one person testing alone.
# ARENA_PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:8003

# The built pitch, when the arena is the thing serving it. Point this at Vite's
# dist/ and the big screen frames the bundle from /pitch/ instead of from an
# origin of its own. Unset locally, where Vite serves the pitch on :5173.
# ARENA_PITCH_DIR=

# Where the pitch is served from. The big screen frames it. The default follows
# ARENA_PITCH_DIR: /pitch when the arena is serving the bundle, :5173 otherwise.
# ARENA_PITCH_URL=http://localhost:5173

# --- The game's agent chain ---------------------------------------------------
# A shout is carried to the game's ADK coach, which hands it to the team captain
# over A2A, which asks the four player agents at once.

# The ADK server, and the application name `adk web .` registers for the
# `agents` package beside it.
# ARENA_COACH_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8000
# ARENA_COACH_APP=agents

# How long one hop of the chain may go quiet before the arena gives up on it.
# The specialists run in parallel and the slowest of them sets this.
# ARENA_COACH_IDLE_SECONDS=90

# How many chains may be talking to Gemini at once, across every room. Sized to
# the quota you have on the day rather than to the hardware.
# ARENA_CHAIN_LIMIT=4

# The whole chain, from the moment it has a slot to the huddle. A match is three
# minutes long, so a shout unanswered inside this is of no further use.
# ARENA_CHAIN_SECONDS=150
